    "q-97641": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97641",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds.",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}+(4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{sin}^{2}⁡4t+48{cos}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48({sin}^{2}⁡4t+{cos}^{2}⁡4t)} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot |4|=4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
