    "q-97669": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97669",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds.",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-50\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i-50\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-50\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)^{2}+(-50\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{50}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡5t+{50}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{50}^{2}} \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $50\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =5\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={5}^{2}\\cdot 2=50\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
